<article class="crs-lesson">
    <h3><span class="crs-lesson-num">Lesson 6.1</span> Handling Objections &amp; Guiding the Decision</h3>

    <span class="crs-sub-label">6.1.1 Final-Hour Jitters Are Normal</span>
    <p>Final objections about price, inspection results, or market timing are rarely about the property itself — they're usually rooted in the fear of making a massive financial commitment. Arguing against the objection almost never works, because you'd be arguing against the wrong thing.</p>

    <span class="crs-sub-label">6.1.2 The LAER Method</span>
    <p>Tap each step to see what it looks like in the room.</p>
    <div class="crs-pin-grid" data-group="m6-laer">
        <button type="button" class="crs-pin is-active" data-target="m6-laer-0">
            <div class="crs-pin-icon"><svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M11 5.882V19.24a1.76 1.76 0 01-3.417.592l-2.147-6.15M18 13a3 3 0 100-6M5.436 13.683A4.001 4.001 0 017 6h1.832c4.1 0 7.625-1.234 9.168-3v14c-1.543-1.766-5.067-3-9.168-3H7a3.988 3.988 0 01-1.564-.317z"/></svg></div>
            <span class="crs-pin-label">Listen</span>
            <svg class="crs-pin-check" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="crs-pin" data-target="m6-laer-1">
            <div class="crs-pin-icon"><svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg></div>
            <span class="crs-pin-label">Acknowledge</span>
            <svg class="crs-pin-check" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="crs-pin" data-target="m6-laer-2">
            <div class="crs-pin-icon"><svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M21 21l-4.35-4.35M11 19a8 8 0 100-16 8 8 0 000 16z"/></svg></div>
            <span class="crs-pin-label">Explore</span>
            <svg class="crs-pin-check" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="crs-pin" data-target="m6-laer-3">
            <div class="crs-pin-icon"><svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M8 12h.01M12 12h.01M16 12h.01M21 12c0 4.418-4.03 8-9 8-1.06 0-2.077-.163-3.02-.465L3 21l1.395-3.72C3.512 16.032 3 14.579 3 13c0-4.418 4.03-8 9-8s9 3.582 9 7z"/></svg></div>
            <span class="crs-pin-label">Respond</span>
            <svg class="crs-pin-check" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>
        </button>
    </div>
    <div class="crs-pin-detail is-open" data-detail="m6-laer-0"><strong>Listen</strong><p>Let the client finish the objection completely, without interrupting or jumping to a rebuttal. Most agents start formulating a response before the client is even done talking — resist that.</p></div>
    <div class="crs-pin-detail" data-detail="m6-laer-1"><strong>Acknowledge</strong><p>Validate the concern as reasonable before responding to it. "That's a fair thing to think about" costs you nothing and lowers the client's defenses immediately.</p></div>
    <div class="crs-pin-detail" data-detail="m6-laer-2"><strong>Explore</strong><p>Ask a follow-up question to find out what's really behind the objection — price, timing, and market fear often disguise a different, more personal worry underneath.</p></div>
    <div class="crs-pin-detail" data-detail="m6-laer-3"><strong>Respond</strong><p>Once you know the real concern, respond with relevant facts — ideally the same needs, budget, and timeline data you gathered back in Module 3.</p></div>

    <p>Notice that "Respond" is the last step, not the first. Most agents skip straight to it, which is why so many final objections turn into arguments instead of resolutions.</p>
    <div class="crs-scenario">
        <strong>🇵🇭 Philippine Scenario</strong>
        <p>A day before signing, your buyer suddenly says, "Maybe we should wait, prices might drop." Instead of arguing, you listen, acknowledge that it's a big decision, and explore what's driving the hesitation — it turns out they're anxious about affordability, not the market. You respond by walking through the amortization numbers you calculated together in Module 3, which addresses the real fear directly.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="crs-takeaway-line"><strong>Core Takeaway:</strong> Final objections are rarely about the property; they are usually rooted in the fear of making a massive financial commitment. Validate their fear, then rely on the data you gathered in Module 3 to reassure them.</div>
</article>

<article class="crs-lesson">
    <h3><span class="crs-lesson-num">Lesson 6.2</span> Completing the Handoff (The Closing Process)</h3>

    <span class="crs-sub-label">6.2.1 The Job Intensifies, Not Ends</span>
    <p>The agent's job intensifies, not ends, once the contract is signed. Think of yourself as the conductor of the deal: no single party is tracking every deadline across every other party — that's your job, and missing one can delay or even collapse the closing.</p>

    <span class="crs-sub-label">6.2.2 Who You're Coordinating</span>
    <p>During escrow, you're the connective tissue between three parties who rarely talk to each other directly.</p>
    <ul class="crs-list">
        <li><strong>Mortgage lenders</strong> — following up on loan approval, requirements, and release timelines.</li>
        <li><strong>Title officers</strong> — making sure title transfer paperwork is moving on schedule.</li>
        <li><strong>Home inspectors</strong> — confirming inspection contingencies are cleared before the closing date.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A simple tracking sheet — one row per open item, one column per party, with a deadline date — is usually all it takes to stay ahead of this. The goal is to never be the reason a deadline gets missed.</p>
    <div class="crs-scenario">
        <strong>🇵🇭 Philippine Scenario</strong>
        <p>Your buyer's bank needs an updated Certificate of Employment before releasing the loan, while the developer needs the signed Contract to Sell before scheduling turnover. You proactively follow up with both sides so neither deadline slips, keeping the whole transaction on schedule instead of finding out about a missed requirement after it's already too late.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="crs-takeaway-line"><strong>Core Takeaway:</strong> The agent's job intensifies after the contract is signed. You are the conductor making sure all third parties hit their deadlines so the deal closes on schedule.</div>
</article>

<article class="crs-lesson">
    <h3><span class="crs-lesson-num">Lesson 6.3</span> Maintaining Long-Term Relationships</h3>

    <span class="crs-sub-label">6.3.1 From "Agent on This Deal" to "Lifelong Advisor"</span>
    <p>The best agents transition from "active agent on this deal" to "lifelong real estate advisor." That shift doesn't happen by accident — it happens because they build a system for staying in touch long after the commission is paid.</p>

    <span class="crs-sub-label">6.3.2 The Post-Close Check-In Schedule</span>
    <p>Tap each milestone to see what to check in about.</p>
    <div class="crs-pin-grid" data-group="m6-checkin">
